{{smaller block|My slipp'ry footing fail'd me; and you ''tript''<br>Just as I slipt.
{{right|''Quarles'', Emblems, ii. 14.}}}}
Hence—5. Figuratively, to make a false movement; err; go wrong; be guilty of an inconsistency or an inaccuracy.

{{smaller block|St. Jerome, whose custom is not to pardon ever easily his adversaries if any where they chance to ''trip'', presseth him as thereby making all sorts of men in the world God's enemies.
{{right|''Hooker'', Eccles. Polity, v. 29.}}}}

{{smaller block|The captain, a wise man, after many endeavours to catch me ''tripping'' in some part of my story, at last began to have a better opinion of my veracity.
{{right|''Swift'', Gulliver's Travels, iv. 11.}}}}

{{smaller block|For Jenny, my cousin, had come to the place, and I knew right well<br>That Jenny had ''tript'' in her time; I knew, but I would not tell.
{{right|''Tennyson'', The Grandmother.}}}}
6. To rush by: said of deer.

{{smaller block|A hundred head of red deer<br>Come ''tripping'' the sheriff full nigh.
{{right|''Robin Hood and the Butcher'' (Child's Ballads, V. 37).}}}}
=Syn. 1. ''Hop'', ''Leap'', etc. See ''skip''<sup>1</sup>.

'''II.''' ''trans.'' 1. To perform with a light or tripping step, as a dance.

{{smaller block|Every maid<br>Fit for this revel was arrayed,<br>The hornpipe neatly ''tripping''.
{{right|''Drayton'', Nymphidia.}}}}
2. To cause to stumble or fall, make a false step, or lose the footing by catching or en-

readings.c2s1:
tangling the feet or suddenly checking their free action: often followed by ''up''.

{{smaller block|A stump doth ''trip'' him in his pace;
Down comes poor Hob upon his face.
{{right|''Drayton'', Nymphidia.}}}}

{{smaller block|Your excuse must be that . . . a mop stood across the entry, and ''tript'' you ''up''.
{{right|''Swift'', Advice to Servants (Footman).}}}}

'''3.''' To cause to stumble by placing an obstruction in the way; hence, to give a wrong turn to, or cause to halt or stumble, by presenting a mental or moral stumbling-block.

{{smaller block|Be you contented, wearing now the garland, . . .
To ''trip'' the course of law and blunt the sword
That guards the peace and safety of your person.
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., v. 2. 87.}}}}

'''4.''' To catch in a fault, offense, or error; detect in a misstep or blunder.

{{smaller block|Yea, what and whosoeuer he be that thinkes himselfe a very good Italian, and that to ''trip'' others.
{{right|''Florio'', It. Dict.. Ep. Ded., p. [5].}}}}

{{smaller block|He must, sir, be
A better statesman than yourself, that can
''Trip'' me in anything; I will not speak
Before these witnesses.
{{right|''Fletcher'' (''and another''), Noble Gentleman, iii. 4.}}}}

'''5.''' ''Naut.'': (''a'') To loose, as an anchor from the bottom by means of its cable or buoy-rope. (''b'') To turn, as a yard, from a horizontal to a vertical position.

{{smaller block|The royal yards were all ''tripped'' and lowered together.
{{right|''R. H. Dana, Jr.'', Before the Mast, p. 218.}}}}

'''6.''' ''Theat.'', to double in the center: said of a drop so situated that there is not room enough to hoist it out of sight.—'''7.''' In ''mech.'': (''a'') To

readings.c2s2:
strike against, as a moving part against an obstruction. (''b'') To release suddenly, as the clutch of the windlass of a pile-driver, or the valve-closing mechanism in the trip-gear of a steam-engine, etc.

'''trip<sup>1</sup>''' (trip), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''tryppe''; < ME. ''trippe'' = Dan. ''trip'', a short step; from the verb.] 1. A light, short step; a lively movement of the feet.

{{smaller block|More fine in ''trip'' then foote of running roe,<br>More pleasant then the field of flowring grasse.
{{right|''England's Helicon'' (1614). (''Nares.'')}}}}

{{smaller block|"Where gang ye, young John," she says,<br>"Sae early in the day?<br>It gars me think by your fast ''trip''<br>Your journey 's far away."
{{right|''The Fause Lover'' (Child's Ballads, IV. 90).}}}}

2. A journey or voyage; an excursion; a jaunt; specifically, in transportation, the performance of service one way over a route, the performance of service both ways being a ''round trip''.

{{smaller block|An aungell . . . bad me flee<br>With hym and the<br>On-to Egipte.<br>And sertis I dred me sore<br>To make my smal ''trippe''. ''York Plays'', p. 142.}}

{{smaller block|She, to return our foreigner's complaisance,<br>At Cupid's call, has made a ''trip'' to France.
{{right|''Farquhar'', Love and a Bottle, Epil.}}}}

{{smaller block|By thus advancing its base of operations on the same line, or by changing from one line to another, the wagons were relieved of two ''trips''.
{{right|''Comte de Paris'', Civil War in America (trans.), I. 213.}}}}

3. A sudden seizure or catch, as that by which

readings.c2s3:
a wrestler throws his antagonist.

{{smaller block|Of good hope no councell thou craue
Til deeth thee caste with a ''trippe'' of dissaite.
{{right|''Hymns to Virgin'', etc. (E. E. T. S.), p. 75.}}}}

{{smaller block|Or, stript for wrestling, smears his limbs with oil,
And watches, with a ''trip'' his foe to foil.
{{right|''Dryden'', tr. of Virgil's Georgics, ii. 776.}}}}

4. A stumble by the loss of foothold or a striking of the foot against an object.—5. In ''mach.'', a hitting of a moving part against some obstruction to its free movement.—6. A failure; an error; a blunder.

{{smaller block|And mad'st imperfect words with childish ''trips'',
Half unpronounced, slide through my infant lips.
{{right|''Milton'', Vacation Exercise, l. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|How, Cousin? I'd have you to know, before this faux pas, this ''Trip'' of mine, the World cou'd not talk of me.
{{right|''Wycherley'', Plain Dealer, v. 1.}}}}

7. In the fisheries, the catch, take, or fare of fish caught during a voyage; the proceeds of a trip in fish.—8. ''Naut.'', a single board or tack in plying to windward. ''Admiral Smyth.''—9. In ''coursing'', an unsuccessful effort of the dogs to kill. ''Encyc. Brit.'', VI. 515.—10. A small arch over a drain. ''Halliwell.''—'''Jonah trip.''' {{smaller|See ''Jonah.''}}—'''Round trip.''' {{smaller|See def. 2.}}—'''To fetch trip,''' {{smaller|to go backward in order to jump the further. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.]}}—'''To hail for a trip.''' {{smaller|See ''hail''<sup>3</sup>.}}=='''Syn. 2.''' {{smaller|''Tour'', ''Travel'', etc. See ''journey''.}}

'''trip'''<sup>2</sup> (trip), ''n.'' [< ME. ''trip'', ''trippe'': supposed to be a var. of ''troop'', or from the same ult. source.] 1. A number of animals (rarely of persons) together; a flock. [Provincial.]

readings.c3s3:
three, + ''partibilis'', divisible: see ''partible'', and cf. ''tripartite''.] In ''bot.'', exhibiting a tendency to split into three parts or divisions.

'''tripartient''' (trī-pär′shient), ''a.'' [< L. ''tres'' (''tri-''), three, + ''partien(t-)s'', ppr. of ''partiri'', divide: see ''part'', ''v.''] Dividing into three parts: said of a number that divides another into three equal parts.

'''tripartite''' (trip′är-tīt or trī-pär′tīt), ''a.'' [< late ME. ''trypartyte'', < OF. (and F.) ''tripartite'' = Pr. ''tripartit'' = Sp. Pg. It. ''tripartito'', < L. ''tripartitus'', ''tripertitus'', divided into three parts, < ''tres'' (''tri-''), three, + ''partitus'', pp. of ''partiri'', part, divide: see ''partite''.] 1. Divided into three parts; three-parted.

{{smaller block|She blazed abroade perdy a people small,<br>Late landed heere, and founde this pleasaunt Ile,<br>And how that now it was diuided all,<br>Made ''tripartite'', and might within a while<br>Bee won by force, by treason, fraud, or guile.
{{right|''Mir. for Mags.'', I. 43.}}}}

{{smaller block|Wisdom is ''tripartite'': saying, doing, avoiding.
{{right|''Landor'', Imag. Conv., Diogenes and Plato.}}}}

{{smaller block|The ''tripartite'' division of government into legislative, executive, and judicial. ''Bancroft'', Hist. Const., II. 327.}}

2. Having three corresponding parts or copies.

{{smaller block|This Indentur ''tripartite'' made the twenty dey of Aprile, the yere of our lorde godd a thowsaunde fyve hundreth and fourteyn. ''English Gilds'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 143.}}

{{smaller block|Our indentures ''tripartite'' are drawn.
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., iii. 1. 80.}}}}

3. Made or concluded between three parties: as, a ''tripartite'' treaty.

{{smaller block|The College, myself, and Mr. Lintot, the bookseller, enter into a ''tripartite'' agreement upon these terms.
{{right|''W. Brome'', Letters of Eminent Men, II. 96.}}}}
